# Reorder.Board

`Reorder.Board` lets items be dragged between the [`Reorder.Group`](/docs/reorder)s inside it: kanban columns, buckets, "selected" and "available" lists. Wrap the groups in a Board and a card can leave one group and join another in the same drag, while both groups' other items animate to make room. It renders no element of its own.

<Example>
  <Kanban />
</Example>

## Usage

Wrap two or more groups in `Reorder.Board`. Each group keeps its own `values` and `onReorder`, exactly as it would on its own:

```svelte
<script lang="ts">
    import { Reorder } from '@humanspeak/svelte-motion'

    let todo = $state(['Sketch', 'Write', 'Review'])
    let done = $state(['Plan'])
</script>

<Reorder.Board>
    <Reorder.Group axis="y" values={todo} onReorder={(next) => (todo = next)}>
        {#each todo as item (item)}
            <Reorder.Item value={item}>{item}</Reorder.Item>
        {/each}
    </Reorder.Group>
    <Reorder.Group axis="y" values={done} onReorder={(next) => (done = next)}>
        {#each done as item (item)}
            <Reorder.Item value={item}>{item}</Reorder.Item>
        {/each}
    </Reorder.Group>
</Reorder.Board>
```

The Board can sit anywhere above the groups, so put whatever layout you need (a flex row of columns, headers, scroll containers) between it and them.

## Moving between groups

When a dragged item's centre crosses into another group, it moves there:

1. The group it left calls `onReorder(without, moved)`, its values without the item.
2. The group it joins calls `onReorder(with, moved)`, its values with the item in the slot under the item's centre.

Both calls pass the moved item's value as the second argument. The drag carries on in the new group, so the item can keep reordering there or move on to a third group.

The two calls come one after the other, so if the app keeps every group in one state, update it from the latest state rather than from a copy taken before the drag. With a `$state` record, writing each group's own key does exactly that:

```svelte
<script lang="ts">
    let columns = $state({ todo: ['a', 'b'], doing: ['c'], done: [] as string[] })
</script>

<Reorder.Board>
    {#each Object.keys(columns) as id (id)}
        <Reorder.Group axis="y" values={columns[id]} onReorder={(next) => (columns[id] = next)}>
            <!-- items -->
        </Reorder.Group>
    {/each}
</Reorder.Board>
```

## canMove

`canMove(value, from, to)` decides whether `value` may move from the group whose values are `from` to the group whose values are `to`. It runs before either group's `onReorder`. Return `false` to refuse: a dragged item stays in its group until it's dragged over one that accepts it, and a keyboard move skips on to the next group in that direction.

```svelte
<script lang="ts">
    const canMove = (card: string, from: string[], to: string[]) =>
        to !== columns.doing || to.length < 3
</script>

<Reorder.Board {canMove}>
    <!-- groups -->
</Reorder.Board>
```

- While a dragged item is over a group it refuses, `canMove` is called every frame. Keep it fast and free of side effects.
- `from` and `to` are the groups' `values` arrays, so compare them with your own state to tell the groups apart.
- Escape ignores `canMove`: it always returns a grabbed item to the group and slot it was grabbed from.

## Keyboard

Board extends [Reorder's keyboard support](/docs/reorder#keyboard) across groups. Tab to an item, then:

| Key | Action |
|-----|--------|
| `Space` | Grab the focused item, or drop it if it's grabbed |
| Arrow keys along the axis (grabbed) | Move the item one place within its group |
| Arrow keys across the axis (grabbed) | Move the item to the nearest group in that direction, e.g. Left/Right between `axis="y"` columns |
| Arrow keys along the axis past either end (grabbed) | Move the item to the nearest group in that direction, e.g. Down from the last item of a column into the group below |
| `Home` / `End` | Move focus, or the grabbed item, to the first or last place in its own group. They never leave the group |
| `Escape` | Return the item to the group and slot it was grabbed from, then drop it |

A move to another group calls both groups' `onReorder`, as a drag does, and the item keeps focus and its grab in the new group. A group that `canMove` refuses is skipped.

## Scrolling

Inside a Board, items drag on both axes, so a card can leave its column sideways, and auto-scroll works on both axes too. Hold a dragged item near the edge of a scroll container and it scrolls:

- **Along the group's axis** (down an `axis="y"` column), the scroll container around the group scrolls, as it does for a single group.
- **Across the axis** (sideways between columns), the scroll container around the columns scrolls, such as a board that scrolls horizontally. The column itself isn't scrolled sideways, even though a card dragged out of it overflows it.

Put the scroll container around a group rather than on the group itself, and give the group a minimum height that fills its column, so an empty column still has an area to drop into:

```svelte
<div style="height: 300px; overflow-y: auto">
    <Reorder.Group axis="y" values={todo} onReorder={(next) => (todo = next)} style="min-height: 100%">
        <!-- items -->
    </Reorder.Group>
</div>
```

## Notes

- Values must be unique across all the Board's groups, not just within each group.
- Groups nested inside each other's items never exchange items, so a Board can hold nested lists without items jumping between levels.
- A virtualized group places an incoming item using its rendered items only; values that aren't rendered keep their place in `values`.
- Every `Reorder.Group` inside the Board joins it automatically: the groups need no extra props.

## Props

### Reorder.Board

| Prop | Type | Default | Description |
|------|------|---------|-------------|
| `canMove` | `(value: V, from: V[], to: V[]) => boolean` | — | Whether `value` may move from the group with values `from` to the group with values `to`. Called before either `onReorder`; return `false` to refuse |

`Reorder.Group` and `Reorder.Item` take their [usual props](/docs/reorder#props).

